+// Orthros (unattributed.patch): the clock's glue (Emscripten 6.0.1: src/lib/libcore.js emscripten_get_now,
+// src/lib/libwasi.js clock_time_get) in this MEMORY64 + pthreads + ALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH build, on every thread.
+// OpenMW reads the clock a few hundred times a frame (osg::Timer::tick: every ScopedProfile, the viewer, the cull/draw
+// stats; std::chrono::steady_clock::now: the terrain preload's time slices, the Lua scripts' real-time clock, mods
+// calling it from every onFrame). Profiled on r13 (Vanilla+ FR, Seyda Neen, tools/openmw/perf.mjs --profile): the
+// clock's JS 0.26 ms a frame, of which emscripten_get_now's own time 0.17 and performance.now() 0.03.
+// Linked after the system libraries (--js-library, link-openmw.sh): its entries replace theirs of the same name, whose
+// __sig, __i53abi, __proxy and __nothrow stay (deps are only added to).
+//
+// Switch: OPENMW_ORTHROS_CLOCK, read once per thread, at its first call once it can be read (a call before that takes
+// the library's path and decides nothing). "0": the library's own bodies, kept below as orthrosClockOrig_<name>;
+// absent or anything else: the paths below. On the page's thread: ENV, once the runtime is initialized (the page fills
+// ENV in preRun, before initRuntime). On a pthread (the OSG pager, the preload and async threads, the whole engine
+// under OMW_PROXY), whose JS ENV is empty: the C environment (getenv), in the shared memory, built from the page's ENV
+// by the constructors on the page's thread before any pthread exists, read once the thread runs (its stack set up):
+// every thread follows the switch, so "0" is the previous code path everywhere.
+//
+// - emscripten_get_now (pthreads): performance.timeOrigin + performance.now(). timeOrigin is a getter into the
+//   browser (a Performance attribute, not a fast API call) and the time origin of a global never changes (High
+//   Resolution Time: it is fixed when the global is created), so it is read once and kept: the same double added to
+//   the same performance.now(), the same result. Headless Chromium, 2,000,000 calls: 430 → 236 ns a call.
+// - clock_time_get: wrote its nanoseconds through (growMemViews(), HEAP64)[ptime / 8] = BigInt(nsec): the shared
+//   memory's buffer getter and a BigInt allocated every call. Now the two 32-bit halves of the same integer, through
+//   a view of the heap kept by this glue (a view of a SharedArrayBuffer stays valid when the memory grows: growth gives
+//   a longer buffer, never a detached one; the current one is taken when a write reaches past its end). nsec is an
+//   integer-valued double in [0, 2^64) there (Math.round of a positive time): hi = floor(nsec / 2^32) and
+//   lo = nsec - hi * 2^32 are exact (a power of two scales exactly; the difference is a multiple of nsec's ulp below
+//   2^32, so representable), the same eight bytes BigInt64Array would store, modulo 2^64 as it stores them (checked on
+//   2.2 million values, the edges included). A pointer not 8-aligned (the library wrote nothing there), an unknown
+//   clock or a monotonic clock missing take the library's own body; a NaN or negative time its write (BigInt). The
+//   same calls through a test program linked with and without this file, its clocks made deterministic, print the same
+//   values (clock_gettime of each clock, std::chrono::steady_clock, emscripten_get_now). Headless Chromium: 522 → 273
+//   ns a call.
+
+(() => {
+  // The library's own bodies, kept under $orthrosClockOrig_<name> (no __sig: they take the arguments already
+  // converted); the replaced entries' deps gain their kept body and the switch. (libwasi.js keeps its functions as
+  // their source text, wrapSyscallFunction's: a string the jsifier emits as a function.)
+  const lib = LibraryManager.library;
+  const kept = {};
+  for (const name of ['emscripten_get_now', 'clock_time_get']) {
+    const body = lib[name];
+    if (typeof body != 'function' && !(typeof body == 'string' && /^\s*(\([^)]*\)\s*=>|function\b)/.test(body))) {
